    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.31407/ijees16.222",
    "abstract": "This study quantifies bioclimatic warming and urban-related thermal amplification in coastal Durrs by comparing a historical baseline with a contemporary period .Using consistent gridded temperatures from ERA5-Land and raster-based spatial modelling, we assess municipality-scale change and within-area heterogeneity.Mean annual temperature increases by 1. 05C (15.34 16.39C).Indicators of reduced maritime moderation show a rise in annual thermal amplitude (15.61 16.72C) and the Gorczyski Continentality Index (19.71 22.58).Local anomaly mapping (Tlocal) reveals strong intra-municipality variability; hotspots defined as Tlocal P90(Tlocal) yield P90 = 0.297C and cover 62.02 km (15.81% of the municipality).Built-up land cover from ESA WorldCover is aggregated to the temperature grid to define an urban mask (built-up fraction 0.10), enabling Urban Thermal Footprint (UTF) accounting via hotspot-urban overlap.The mapped urban area is 348.36 km, while the UTF covers 21.77 km (5.55% of the total area; 6.25% of mapped urban area), indicating that the strongest anomalies cluster in specific urban locations rather than uniformly across built-up space.Correlation/regression diagnostics further indicate spatially structured anomaly patterns (Tshift vs. baseline temperature: Pearson r = -0.932,p = 0.0212; R 2 = 0.868).Overall, the workflow offers a reproducible framework for hotspot/UTF mapping to support targeted heat-mitigation planning and climate-sensitive urban monitoring in a coastal Mediterranean setting.",
    "field": "Urban climatology",
    "problem": "Urban climate assessments often struggle to resolve fine-scale thermal heterogeneity, failing to distinguish broad regional warming from localized urban-driven amplification, which is critical for targeted heat-mitigation planning in coastal cities.",
    "method": "This study quantifies bioclimatic warming and urban thermal hotspots in coastal Durr\u00ebs by comparing gridded ERA5-Land temperatures across historical (1971-2000) and contemporary (2001-2025) periods, using spatial anomaly modeling and land cover data to delineate an Urban Thermal Footprint and assess changes in continentality.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify coastal warming and urban thermal hotspots in Durr\u00ebs, Albania, by comparing gridded temperature data from a historical baseline (1971\u20132000) with a contemporary period (2001\u20132025) to assess climate change and urban heat effects.",
